WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.133
Source 2: expert-written cases.

2
00:00:02.413 --> 00:00:17.005
A domain expert can write cases that logs don’t contain yet: the policy edge (an annual plan on day 14 versus day 15), the rule that only applies to one plan, the request that looks routine but must be escalated.

3
00:00:17.285 --> 00:00:22.917
These are often the most valuable cases per line, because each one encodes a piece of product knowledge.

4
00:00:23.197 --> 00:00:32.115
Anthropic’s evals post recommends writing unambiguous tasks with reference solutions, and letting domain experts contribute tasks directly.

5
00:00:32.395 --> 00:00:44.021
In practice that means a simple format the expert can edit (a spreadsheet or a YAML file reviewed in a pull request), one behavior per case, and a short note on why the case exists.

6
00:00:44.301 --> 00:00:54.499
The note matters more than it seems: six months later, someone will ask whether a failing case is still right, and “added after the March chargeback incident” answers it.

7
00:00:54.779 --> 00:00:57.552
Experts are also your check on the other two sources.

8
00:00:57.832 --> 00:01:03.400
Have them review a sample of log-derived and synthetic cases for wrong expected outputs.

9
00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:10.976
A test set with a wrong answer key will punish the system for being right, and you won’t notice until someone reads the failures.
